          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If your nose bleeds every winter, dry indoor air is the likely cause, and a bedroom humidifier held at 40 to 50 percent is one of the simplest ways to address it. Pair it with a thin layer of petroleum jelly or saline inside the nose and keep hands away from the crusts.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Use proper first aid when a bleed happens: sit up, lean forward and pinch for 10 to 15 minutes. And if nosebleeds come more than once a week, follow an injury or will not stop within 30 minutes, see a doctor. Humidity fixes dry air, not everything else.
          </p>
